===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|countable}} A fragment of a human expression that is repeated by somebody else, for example from [[literature]] or a famous [[speech]].
#: {{syn|en|citation|cite#Noun|quote#Noun}}
#: {{ux|en|"Where they burn books, they will also burn people" is a famous '''quotation''' from {{w|Heinrich Heine}}.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of [[quote#Verb|quoting]] someone or something.
#: {{syn|en|citation}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=[[w:Samuel Baker|S[amuel] W[hite] Baker]]|chapter=Arrival at Metemma, or Gallabat|title=Exploration of the Nile Tributaries of Abyssinia. The Sources, Supply, and Overflow of the Nile; the Country, People, Customs, etc.{{nb...|Interspersed with Highly Exciting Adventures of the Author. Among Elephants, Lions, Buffaloes, Hippopotami, Rhinoceros, etc. Accompanied by Expert Native Sword Hunters. Illustrated. By Sir S. W. Baker, M.&nbsp;A., F.&nbsp;R.&nbsp;G.&nbsp;S. […] With a Supplementary Sketch Relative to the Captivity and Release of English Subjects and the Career of the Late Emperor Theodore. By Rev. W. L. Gage.}}|location=Hartford, Conn.|publisher={{...|Published by}} O[rlando] D[wight] Case & Co.|year=1868|page=523|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/explorationofnil00bakeuoft/page/523/mode/1up|passage=One of these preachers was a blacksmith, whose iron constitution had entirely given way, and the little strength that remained he exhausted in endless '''quotation''' of texts from the Bible.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=H[enry] W[alcott] Boynton|title=Books New and Old|journal=[[w:The Atlantic|The Atlantic Monthly: A Magazine of Literature, Science, Art, and Politics]]|volume=XCVI|issue=6|location=Boston, Mass.; New York, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Houghton Mifflin Harcourt|Houghton, Mifflin and Company]]; Cambridge, Mass.: [[w:Riverside Insights|The Riverside Press]]|month=December|year=1905|section=section I|page=844|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/atlantic96bostuoft/atlantic96bostuoft/page/n851/mode/1up|columns=1–2|passage=Mr. [[w:Augustine Birrell|[Augustine] Birrell]] has, as usual, quoted very liberally, and to excellent effect. '''Quotation''' is an art the difficulty of which may easily be underrated by paragraph-writers or by persons who do not write at all. You may say no end of wise things about a writer, and yet fail to convey a sense of the peculiar flavor for which you really value him. To insinuate a phrase or verse of our author into the midst of our own readable (because ephemeral) discourse, is all most of us may do, without giving our readers an unpalatable suspicion that they are being seduced into the perusal of a real author. Mr. Birrell quotes by the page, and we gratefully read every line because we are sure Mr. Birrell, at least, is incapable of asking us to read anything inconsequent or dull.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=David Tsang|entry=UNG, Chinary|editors=[[w:Brian Morton (Scottish writer)|Brian Morton]]; Pamela Collins|title=Contemporary Composers|location=Chicago, Ill.; London|publisher=St. James Press|year=1992|page=936|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/contemporarycomp0000unse_m5n3/page/936/mode/1up|column=1|isbn=1-55862-085-0|passage=In his mature works, [[w:Chinary Ung|[Chinary] Ung]] pays regular homage to Cambodian music by evoking its ambience without resorting to '''quotation''' of specific Cambodian melodies.}}
# {{lb|en|countable}} A price that has been quoted for buying or selling.
#: {{ux|en|Let's get a '''quotation''' for repairing the roof before we decide whether it's worth doing.}}
# {{lb|en|uncountable}} The act of setting a [[price]].
# {{lb|en|countable|obsolete}} A [[quota]], a [[share]].<ref>{{R:Webster 1913|Quotation}}</ref>

